# Shared static mutable state needs a mutex

A function-local `static` container or a file-scope flag is process-lifetime shared memory. C++11 magic-static init is thread-safe **once**. Later reads and writes of a `QHash`, `bool`, or similar from `defaultFor` / `setUserOverride` / background export are a data race unless one mutex (or RW lock) covers the whole critical section.

- Put the mutex **in the same struct** as the data it guards (`UserOverrideState`, `callbackEntriesMutex` next to the vector). Do not leave a bare `static QHash` and a separate `bool loaded`.
- Hold the lock for the entire lookup or update. Do not `constFind` under one lock and `cend()` after release (iterator invalidation / torn read).
- Helpers that touch the cache (`loadOverridesFromSettings`) must assume the caller already holds the lock. Do not take the same `std::mutex` again (it is not recursive).
- Serialize `QSettings` load/save for those keys under the same lock. `QSettings` is not safe for the same file from two threads.
- Immutable after init (`const static` factory tables) does not need a mutex. Concurrent readers plus a writer still do.
- If you must call out (Qt signals, user callbacks), snapshot under the lock, then invoke **outside** it (`snapshotCallbackEntries` in SystemPowerNotifier).

```cpp
// BAD — static hash + flag; defaultFor races setUserOverride
QHash<QString, EventDuration>& userOverrides() {
  static QHash<QString, EventDuration> overrides;
  return overrides;
}
bool overridesLoaded = false;

// GOOD — one struct, one lock, one critical section
struct UserOverrideState {
  std::mutex mutex;
  QHash<QString, EventDuration> overrides;
  bool loaded = false;
};
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(state.mutex);
loadOverridesFromSettings(state);
const auto iterator = state.overrides.constFind(key);
```
